1
00:00:00,942 --> 00:00:09,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what you call you, your perspective, your discernment, your recognition, is based on the thing already being there.

2
00:00:10,447 --> 00:00:11,508
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the other way around.

3
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:17,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like the you observer that's here, watching it, and then it goes away.

4
00:00:18,231 --> 00:00:29,157
[SPEAKER_00]: The observer is there because of the presence of the mental states, which means, if that mental state goes away, that observer... like, it just... it doesn't apply anymore.

5
00:00:58,188 --> 00:00:58,928
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe we could...

6
00:00:58,948 --> 00:01:16,277
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know, like, explore, like, what's the... Like, I heard that quite a number of times, like, you know, like, oh, I have a lot of anger, but I don't have any lust, or I have a lot of, kind of, restlessness, but I don't have any doubt, or things like that.

7
00:01:17,037 --> 00:01:21,259
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh, some things that I've been contemplating is to see that, try to see that

8
00:01:22,674 --> 00:01:40,237
[SPEAKER_02]: The relationship between the different hindrances, and it's not about, like, the specificity about one or the other, but it's more about, like, how I choose to act, and how that, kind of, opens the door,

9
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:45,232
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the common ground?

10
00:01:45,272 --> 00:01:52,638
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of how you experience it, the common ground between experiencing one hindrance, experiencing anger, experiencing anxiety,

11
00:02:29,094 --> 00:02:38,317
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, perhaps we should start out by saying that, as long as you don't act out, can we speak of a hindrance?

12
00:02:39,497 --> 00:02:46,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but the thing is, you don't see the full extent of acting out initially.

13
00:02:46,439 --> 00:02:51,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, your mind is pressuring you to do something.

14
00:02:53,161 --> 00:02:55,821
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are not peaceful.

15
00:02:56,462 --> 00:02:57,122
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite obvious.

16
00:02:58,562 --> 00:03:03,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to that end, your perspective also is constricted, which is the main... like, you don't see things clearly.

17
00:03:04,064 --> 00:03:06,485
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the main aspect of the hindrance.

18
00:03:06,865 --> 00:03:14,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you may not be acting out of the hindrance, but you can't tell apart left and right, basically.

19
00:03:15,147 --> 00:03:24,851
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that you're not, like, making it worse by acting, but your mind is basically sorting in doubt or whatever.

20
00:03:25,711 --> 00:03:26,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

21
00:03:28,472 --> 00:03:32,316
[SPEAKER_03]: And now the question is, what is it that all the five things have in common?

22
00:03:32,696 --> 00:03:39,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because then you would realize that you can't actually have only doubt and no anger, or only anger and no doubt.

23
00:03:39,542 --> 00:03:39,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

24
00:03:40,022 --> 00:03:42,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Only sensuality and no ill-will or something.

25
00:03:42,765 --> 00:03:43,025
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

26
00:04:00,276 --> 00:04:06,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically, just recall one time when you were experiencing... the most recent time that you were experiencing a hindrance.

27
00:04:08,187 --> 00:04:14,596
[SPEAKER_01]: What was the... the thing that preceded the hindrance, the particular...

28
00:04:17,603 --> 00:04:24,844
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the characteristics of that hindrance that would have been in common to all other times that you also experienced hindrances?

29
00:04:25,124 --> 00:04:27,465
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it does involve a loss of perspective.

30
00:04:28,605 --> 00:04:35,727
[SPEAKER_03]: By so being identified, let's say, with a content and not seeing a hindrance as a hindrance, that's when you're hindered.

31
00:04:35,987 --> 00:04:42,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But is that loss of perspective just a... just because you forgot something that you...

32
00:04:43,168 --> 00:04:44,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Could I just remember?

33
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:48,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is that rooted in something deeper than that lack of perspective?

34
00:04:48,253 --> 00:04:49,034
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah.

35
00:04:49,755 --> 00:04:50,115
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

36
00:04:50,276 --> 00:04:52,458
[SPEAKER_04]: I guess you're taking something for granted.

37
00:04:53,539 --> 00:04:54,480
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not even like you're not...

38
00:04:57,253 --> 00:04:59,455
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not necessarily there for you to recollect.

39
00:05:00,416 --> 00:05:18,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but if you... of course, taking things for granted is a byproduct of being hindered, but because you... like, from that perspective of where you are now, hindered, you could say, okay, I won't take things for granted anymore, but you'll still feel...

40
00:05:20,072 --> 00:05:22,693
[SPEAKER_01]: the weight of that hindrance.

41
00:05:22,773 --> 00:05:28,074
[SPEAKER_00]: And you still won't really... like you... because you would say, okay, let me not take things for granted.

42
00:05:28,334 --> 00:05:34,775
[SPEAKER_00]: You would recall the same ideas that before used to be the insights that you had developed or whatever.

43
00:05:35,536 --> 00:05:39,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, because of the state you're in, it makes no difference.

44
00:05:39,136 --> 00:05:41,317
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, because you're not seeing your

45
00:05:43,827 --> 00:05:47,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but is it only that something that you're not seeing, or is it more than that?

46
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:54,854
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the loss of the perspective is due to the previous acting out.

47
00:05:56,214 --> 00:05:58,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but...

48
00:05:59,856 --> 00:06:04,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the previous acting out, what is the most immediate result of it?

49
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:08,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Before the loss of perspective.

50
00:06:08,501 --> 00:06:10,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Before in a structural sense, let's say, not in time.

51
00:06:13,524 --> 00:06:14,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Ah, in a structural sense.

52
00:06:31,376 --> 00:06:32,897
[SPEAKER_01]: How does it feel, basically?

53
00:06:37,461 --> 00:06:39,602
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's what we all already said.

54
00:06:39,642 --> 00:06:40,783
[SPEAKER_03]: Your mind begins to pressure you.

55
00:06:41,452 --> 00:06:41,692
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

56
00:06:42,593 --> 00:06:44,414
[SPEAKER_03]: Towards engagement, basically.

57
00:06:45,254 --> 00:06:47,275
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, which is always with the contents.

58
00:06:47,736 --> 00:06:51,678
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you go along with that, that is the same as losing the perspective.

59
00:06:52,398 --> 00:06:52,619
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

60
00:06:52,659 --> 00:06:54,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's pushing towards engagement.

61
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:58,402
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is, like, eh...

62
00:07:00,363 --> 00:07:00,983
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

63
00:07:01,584 --> 00:07:04,485
[SPEAKER_00]: We want to see what is the common ground between all of them.

64
00:07:05,345 --> 00:07:14,510
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, you might say, okay, especially towards this particular engagement, and then you might think, okay, if I just don't do that, I'm not engaging.

65
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:15,410
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

66
00:07:15,450 --> 00:07:16,571
[SPEAKER_03]: But it is more general.

67
00:07:16,631 --> 00:07:19,632
[SPEAKER_03]: It is like, from that point of view, any engagement.

68
00:07:20,232 --> 00:07:21,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

69
00:07:21,413 --> 00:07:23,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you want to see.

70
00:07:23,254 --> 00:07:25,615
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see that pressure as such,

71
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:34,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Before it even becomes a specific engagement, then you can see that that's what the common ground is.

72
00:07:35,023 --> 00:07:41,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how you would fail to actually deal with the problem.

73
00:07:41,125 --> 00:07:44,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you think in terms of, okay, sensuality.

74
00:07:45,086 --> 00:07:47,367
[SPEAKER_00]: And, okay, it's pressuring towards engagement there.

75
00:07:47,807 --> 00:07:48,807
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as long as I don't do...

76
00:07:49,048 --> 00:07:49,848
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't go there,

77
00:07:50,748 --> 00:07:51,469
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not engaging.

78
00:07:52,129 --> 00:08:02,138
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could be doing a million other things, still, out of pressure, and that means that the common ground of the hindrances is being maintained.

79
00:08:02,158 --> 00:08:03,238
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just kind of...

80
00:08:06,611 --> 00:08:17,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you have five empty glasses and you're just kind of emptying, moving the contents from one to the other, but you're not actually getting rid of the water.

81
00:08:18,941 --> 00:08:22,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you want to see.

82
00:08:22,885 --> 00:08:28,149
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the pressure, in that most general sense, mind that is pressured.

83
00:08:29,330 --> 00:08:33,674
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's something that everybody has experienced.

84
00:08:33,834 --> 00:08:34,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like...

85
00:08:37,708 --> 00:08:58,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Just imagine, even before you started practicing or something, occasions where you were just completely doing whatever you wanted, just, I don't know, partying or careless conversation, unwholesome activities, and then, at the end of it,

86
00:08:59,855 --> 00:09:05,681
[SPEAKER_00]: You find yourself doing things, saying things, that afterwards you regret.

87
00:09:06,701 --> 00:09:11,066
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the time, it didn't feel... you didn't see any problem with it.

88
00:09:13,326 --> 00:09:22,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you might even have, like, shouted at somebody, said something very mean to them, and then you go, and afterwards, you regain perspective.

89
00:09:23,231 --> 00:09:24,751
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, oh, sorry for what I did.

90
00:09:24,851 --> 00:09:26,772
[SPEAKER_01]: That was actually stupid.

91
00:09:26,952 --> 00:09:28,393
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the time, you didn't see it.

92
00:09:29,314 --> 00:09:30,474
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the... Why?

93
00:09:30,514 --> 00:09:34,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you were caught up in that pressure in the moment.

94
00:09:34,356 --> 00:09:41,079
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the... that's the practical way to understand how, when the suttas say,

95
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:52,072
[SPEAKER_00]: A mind that is pressured by hindrances doesn't see what's for its own benefit, doesn't see what's for the benefit of others, or for the benefit of both.

96
00:09:52,953 --> 00:09:55,035
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in some kind of...

97
00:09:57,023 --> 00:10:00,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Mystical sense, like it doesn't see reality as it really is or something.

98
00:10:00,506 --> 00:10:08,151
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's... it's... it's the... it's just... oh, this thing that I'm doing is actually harmful for me.

99
00:10:08,191 --> 00:10:08,831
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that simple.

100
00:10:09,452 --> 00:10:21,000
[SPEAKER_00]: And... and it extends to... to... if you take the hindrance in the most fundamental sense, because craving as a whole, appropriation as a whole,

101
00:10:22,629 --> 00:10:32,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed, aversion and delusion as a whole, are things that you also do, which are not in your benefit, which are not in the benefit of others, and not in the benefit of both.

102
00:10:33,001 --> 00:10:40,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by being pressured by hindrances, you wouldn't... because of being pressured by hindrances, you don't see yourself

103
00:10:42,554 --> 00:10:56,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking things as mine, being, you know, involved with the aggregates, involved with the sense bases, on that most, kind of, acute level.

104
00:10:56,887 --> 00:11:07,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, that's why then, you can't... it's not a matter of choice, to like, oh, just try to understand these things, by kind of figuring them out, without

105
00:11:09,202 --> 00:11:17,209
[SPEAKER_00]: First, purifying my conduct, because that's what this is to say, leads to the five hindrances, is misconduct by body, speech, and mind.

106
00:11:17,990 --> 00:11:22,894
[SPEAKER_01]: And misconduct not in a sense of, like, some external moralistic sense.

107
00:11:23,014 --> 00:11:25,296
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just anything that's rooted in greed, aversion, and delusion.

108
00:11:25,437 --> 00:11:29,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that's going to make your mind wild.

109
00:11:29,901 --> 00:11:34,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, you take a child or something, and you just let it do whatever it wants to do.

110
00:11:36,382 --> 00:11:40,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Every little desire that comes up, you allow it.

111
00:11:41,345 --> 00:11:45,207
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, afterwards, try to explain to him that doing his homework is good for him.

112
00:11:47,248 --> 00:11:47,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to work.

113
00:11:48,628 --> 00:11:52,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to look at it that way.

114
00:11:52,610 --> 00:11:53,631
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even like...

115
00:11:55,310 --> 00:12:02,452
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the domain where logic or kind of reasoning has a very limited impact.

116
00:12:02,812 --> 00:12:14,376
[SPEAKER_00]: At best, the reasoning could kind of remind you of like, okay, I have to restrain myself and stop feeding this, so that eventually it will calm down.

117
00:12:14,436 --> 00:12:19,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to actually recognize, well, I created this now and

118
00:12:20,910 --> 00:12:30,515
[SPEAKER_00]: First of all, my first concern shouldn't be like, in the moment, just push it away as quickly as possible, because that's, again, still going to be acting out of hindrances.

119
00:12:32,176 --> 00:12:47,363
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... now it's like, I need to stop making it worse, and then, basically, keep in mind that the perspective will only really return when that pressure dissipates, if I don't act out of it for long enough.

120
00:12:49,783 --> 00:12:53,913
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... it's eh... it's eh... that's what the training is.

121
00:12:54,073 --> 00:12:57,040
[SPEAKER_00]: And it takes place on different levels of subtlety.

122
00:12:57,101 --> 00:12:57,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

123
00:12:58,652 --> 00:13:07,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, what I mentioned before was like a very coarse example where you would be actually doing things with your body or saying things.

124
00:13:07,678 --> 00:13:09,620
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're keeping the precepts, they wouldn't go that far.

125
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:23,469
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, there's plenty of room for you to basically become careless and do things that... not seeing at that time... See, because that's a problem.

126
00:13:23,509 --> 00:13:26,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you are not under a hindrance,

127
00:13:27,962 --> 00:13:33,658
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see... you don't have the foresight to see where you're going to end up.

128
00:13:36,462 --> 00:13:37,682
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep going in that direction.

129
00:13:38,162 --> 00:13:40,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Because right now, it doesn't hurt that much.

130
00:13:40,463 --> 00:13:41,583
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind isn't too...

131
00:13:42,303 --> 00:13:44,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you think, oh, well, I'll handle it.

132
00:13:44,424 --> 00:13:45,044
[SPEAKER_00]: It'll be fine.

133
00:13:45,364 --> 00:13:45,624
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

134
00:13:46,704 --> 00:13:47,864
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a little bit.

135
00:13:48,004 --> 00:13:49,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Just for this moment, I'll just do it.

136
00:13:49,905 --> 00:13:51,645
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just give in to this pressure.

137
00:13:51,965 --> 00:13:58,727
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, because you might actually be capable of handling it, but you don't see how you are already in the background agitating your mind.

138
00:13:59,367 --> 00:14:02,528
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, at some point, it begins to bite you and then it's too late.

139
00:14:02,628 --> 00:14:04,308
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, you can handle it right now.

140
00:14:04,929 --> 00:14:07,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind... or at least so you think.

141
00:14:07,674 --> 00:14:10,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, if you were able to handle it, then why did you do it?

142
00:14:11,382 --> 00:14:13,767
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were able to handle the pressure, if you were...

143
00:14:14,650 --> 00:14:18,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, a quantumist words, and you were not moved by it, then why did you give in?

144
00:14:19,975 --> 00:14:28,802
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, it's actually just a kind of secondary... you don't even see your mind, basically.

145
00:14:29,022 --> 00:14:30,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you would see, oh, wait.

146
00:14:31,184 --> 00:14:37,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you wouldn't be able to overlook that direct relationship of, like, pretty much taming yourself.

147
00:14:38,930 --> 00:14:40,031
[SPEAKER_00]: to this wild beast.

148
00:14:40,831 --> 00:14:45,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you know, nobody's gonna come to save you.

149
00:14:45,714 --> 00:14:54,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's... it's... that's why what matters most is like, yeah, inevitably you will get caught up.

150
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:59,363
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you won't... maybe sometimes not even recognize, like, oh, I was actually being careless there.

151
00:15:00,602 --> 00:15:17,951
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you find yourself now with a pressuring state of mind of doubt or aversion or ill-will, you don't want to just find a cure for it in the moment, which is what most people tend to think the practice about.

152
00:15:18,112 --> 00:15:24,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Just use method techniques to put out the fire that I created through my own behavior.

153
00:15:25,476 --> 00:15:29,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, I delight in, like, just having the perfect fire extinguisher

154
00:15:30,659 --> 00:15:38,068
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you actually want to think about is like...

155
00:15:38,128 --> 00:15:42,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so, I ended up with this pressuring state of mind of doubts...

156
00:15:43,873 --> 00:15:48,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever, sad mind, depressed mind, and...

157
00:15:49,535 --> 00:15:58,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, let's say, it will obviously have to go away at some point, because these things are impermanent, unless you keep feeding them somehow.

158
00:15:59,537 --> 00:16:03,678
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, then you want to actually question yourself, okay, but why did this happen?

159
00:16:05,199 --> 00:16:11,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And ten times out of ten, you will see there was a pattern there of things that you were doing.

160
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:12,661
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't happen by accident.

161
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:53,894
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you... when you are pressured by a hindrance, how do you know if you can think about certain contexts and themes to re-establish perspective, or if that's not the right direction to go at the time?

162
00:16:54,595 --> 00:16:56,596
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're always pressured by a hindrance?

163
00:17:00,219 --> 00:17:01,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not a... it's not a...

164
00:17:04,615 --> 00:17:12,980
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm trying to discern those patterns that you just said, in the midst of being pressured by the mind, is that what you're saying?

165
00:17:13,020 --> 00:17:15,321
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, but what pattern do you mean?

166
00:17:15,561 --> 00:17:20,724
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I'm being pressured by this hindrance, and it's because of my past actions.

167
00:17:20,884 --> 00:17:22,045
[SPEAKER_04]: What were those past actions?

168
00:17:22,465 --> 00:17:23,146
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you mean like that?

169
00:17:23,926 --> 00:17:27,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, even that could be helpful, but... Not necessarily.

170
00:17:27,708 --> 00:17:28,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

171
00:17:28,188 --> 00:17:28,689
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

172
00:17:28,729 --> 00:17:29,409
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you know?

173
00:17:35,211 --> 00:17:38,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you could also fall into a trap of saying like, okay, so there's a hindrance now.

174
00:17:39,352 --> 00:17:40,432
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not do anything.

175
00:17:40,492 --> 00:17:50,994
[SPEAKER_00]: I just sit here and grind my teeth and just wait for... and not seeing that that attitude right there could be rooted in the same pressure.

176
00:17:51,054 --> 00:17:51,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

177
00:17:54,335 --> 00:17:55,995
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's basically also the answer.

178
00:17:56,395 --> 00:17:59,096
[SPEAKER_03]: So you want to see that...

179
00:17:59,556 --> 00:18:03,557
[SPEAKER_03]: So you want to take into account your intentions for establishing that context again.

180
00:18:04,779 --> 00:18:05,019
[SPEAKER_03]: Right?

181
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:07,021
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what would be the right intention?

182
00:18:09,704 --> 00:18:18,854
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the right... part of the right intention is to not immediately want to get rid of the hindrance now, for example, because then you're quite liable to fall into ill-will.

183
00:18:20,035 --> 00:18:23,839
[SPEAKER_03]: But at the same time, you also want to maintain some perspective, otherwise you fall into doubt.

184
00:18:23,859 --> 00:18:24,040
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

185
00:18:27,402 --> 00:18:28,202
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like training.

186
00:18:28,222 --> 00:18:38,546
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're going to do it wrong quite a few times, but then also, at some point, you will basically see that this perspective is now established again.

187
00:18:39,906 --> 00:18:41,647
[SPEAKER_03]: But this is all mental work, right?

188
00:18:41,667 --> 00:18:46,568
[SPEAKER_03]: We are already assuming that this is not about talking to yourself, or just, oh, shall I go for a walk or not?

189
00:18:46,608 --> 00:18:48,429
[SPEAKER_03]: That's how it starts in the beginning, but at some point...

190
00:18:49,457 --> 00:18:55,179
[SPEAKER_03]: It is... it just becomes completely... because they are fundamentally mental.

191
00:18:55,379 --> 00:18:55,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:18:56,199 --> 00:18:56,740
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

193
00:18:57,040 --> 00:19:10,585
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, so... what is it that you would need to recognize in order to do that work on the mental level without, as you said, trying to get rid of the hindrance?

194
00:19:10,645 --> 00:19:10,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

195
00:19:12,305 --> 00:19:16,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Often, to most people, it could sound as if you say, OK, don't try to get rid of the hindrance.

196
00:19:16,947 --> 00:19:21,750
[SPEAKER_01]: It means like, I just let myself be carried by it.

197
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:26,632
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you have whatever sensual hindrances or ill will.

198
00:19:26,992 --> 00:19:37,678
[SPEAKER_00]: And to you, it might sound like not trying to get rid of the hindrance means like, OK, so I just let that thinking continue and basically just

199
00:19:39,802 --> 00:19:44,647
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but from this point of view, the thinking might already be sort of a by-product.

200
00:19:45,107 --> 00:19:45,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

201
00:19:45,968 --> 00:19:48,050
[SPEAKER_00]: But you do need to recognize something.

202
00:19:49,524 --> 00:19:52,586
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to rightly not try to get rid of the hindrance.

203
00:19:53,106 --> 00:20:12,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, there's a difference between just shirking your responsibility and saying, okay, well, I'm not going to try to get rid of the hindrance, and rightly not trying to get rid of the hindrance in a way that actually started it and eventually brings it to an end.

204
00:20:12,858 --> 00:20:15,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would be that recognition that's required there?

205
00:20:24,008 --> 00:20:37,667
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I think it's the recognition of the extent of the pressure, when you kind of... when like the line between the pressure and me, quote-unquote, it's kind of clear, like regardless of

206
00:20:38,966 --> 00:20:43,832
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever is coming, you know, like, it cannot pass this point, regardless of what's coming.

207
00:20:43,892 --> 00:20:51,702
[SPEAKER_02]: And if that is very clear, then you can recollect, recollect things that are already present, like the body or whatever.

208
00:20:52,263 --> 00:20:56,588
[SPEAKER_02]: But if this line is not so clear, it's kind of like a sort of grey, you

209
00:20:58,608 --> 00:21:09,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you find yourself in the gray and whatever you are doing there, regardless if like trying to recollect the body or maraṇasati or whatever, if this line is not established first, then... You're just spinning.

210
00:21:09,652 --> 00:21:10,432
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just... yeah.

211
00:21:10,492 --> 00:21:14,754
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I think that the thing is that to recognize this line first.

212
00:21:15,254 --> 00:21:15,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

213
00:21:15,814 --> 00:21:16,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to recognize

214
00:21:18,153 --> 00:21:32,978
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you have to come to the point where you realize nothing will... nothing bad will actually happen here, unless I do intentionally give in to this pressure.

215
00:21:33,298 --> 00:21:34,058
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you would...

216
00:21:35,379 --> 00:21:40,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Practically recognize, like, that you can endure it infinitely, quote-unquote.

217
00:21:40,882 --> 00:21:55,312
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, that has to be based on the recognition, like you said, of that line, such that even if it stays there forever, at no point will I actually do something unwholesome on account of it.

218
00:21:55,693 --> 00:22:00,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, the precepts have to be established, but even on the mental level,

219
00:22:02,037 --> 00:22:18,749
[SPEAKER_00]: There is that work to discerning at which point having the mental pressure of, let's say, sensuality or ill-will, at what point that becomes you thinking out of sensuality and in a way which the Buddha said you should never do.

220
00:22:18,849 --> 00:22:30,737
[SPEAKER_01]: He said like... and that's the confusion that often... like when we talk about, oh, we have to endure the sensuality, the mental state of sensuality, the mental state of ill-will.

221
00:22:31,837 --> 00:22:44,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Then people would quote the sutta saying, where the Buddha says, oh, but as soon as a thought of sensuality, or a thought of ill will, or a thought of cruelty arises, you should obliterate it, eradicate it, and bring it to an end.

222
00:22:45,304 --> 00:22:47,225
[SPEAKER_01]: But those are two completely different things.

223
00:22:47,366 --> 00:22:49,867
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the thoughts are things that you do.

224
00:22:50,547 --> 00:22:53,189
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of your choice to to attend

225
00:22:54,289 --> 00:23:09,317
[SPEAKER_00]: To these mental images, basically, and the pressure, the actual pressure of whatever hindrance it is, that's something that's, even if you wanted to, at best, you could look away from it.

226
00:23:09,997 --> 00:23:10,918
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be there anyway.

227
00:23:12,078 --> 00:23:13,559
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, that's the crucial distinction.

228
00:23:13,579 --> 00:23:19,302
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to clearly discern, okay, this is the mind of whatever hindrance.

229
00:23:19,782 --> 00:23:20,783
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the mind of doubt.

230
00:23:21,503 --> 00:23:22,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the line where

231
00:23:25,029 --> 00:23:29,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaving it be, becomes actually acting out of doubt mentally.

232
00:23:31,192 --> 00:23:33,894
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you can...

233
00:23:34,334 --> 00:23:41,017
[SPEAKER_03]: Restrain those thoughts of doubt, which is then the right endurance of the hindrance of doubt.

234
00:23:41,037 --> 00:23:47,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and practically speaking, that's really all you need to do, because it's fueled, as the Buddha said.

235
00:23:47,701 --> 00:23:48,441
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a fire

236
00:23:51,870 --> 00:24:04,615
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason it keeps going is because you have been throwing sticks and whatever, cow dung or things that are suitable for that fire to keep going.

237
00:24:04,836 --> 00:24:09,297
[SPEAKER_00]: But most of the time, really, you don't recognize yourself doing that, which is a problem.

238
00:24:10,098 --> 00:24:12,699
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you recognize it, you see, okay, well,

239
00:24:14,587 --> 00:24:24,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it were to last however long, you wouldn't feel that burden of being, like, lost.

240
00:24:25,311 --> 00:24:27,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's already a regaining of perspective.

241
00:24:28,692 --> 00:24:36,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Just seeing, like, okay, so it doesn't really matter if it stays, as long as I don't get involved with it and make it worse.

242
00:24:37,595 --> 00:24:37,915
[SPEAKER_01]: And then,

243
00:24:39,840 --> 00:24:40,721
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

244
00:24:40,861 --> 00:24:41,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stay there.

245
00:24:42,803 --> 00:24:51,351
[SPEAKER_00]: If it helps you to recollect the body or recollect feelings or whichever other thing, you would do it now.

246
00:24:52,863 --> 00:25:06,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Maintaining perspective of that line, you wouldn't do it, like, with the same impetus of, like, I'm going to do this so that it... so I can push it away quicker or something, out of impatience, which is still within the hindrances.

247
00:25:08,238 --> 00:25:09,899
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, oh, when is it going to go away?

248
00:25:10,119 --> 00:25:14,644
[SPEAKER_00]: If that question arises, you realize, well, I shouldn't

249
00:25:15,409 --> 00:25:25,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Go in that direction, because that's again going to... Like, why would I worry about that, unless I started to overlook the fact that I'm not actually...

250
00:25:25,816 --> 00:25:29,498
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing unwholesome going to happen here, unless I get involved.

251
00:25:29,518 --> 00:25:29,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

252
00:25:30,339 --> 00:25:32,821
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be no reason to worry about how long it's going to last.

253
00:25:32,941 --> 00:25:38,965
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, which is again, if you get into that, it's going to become either doubt or restlessness, for example.

254
00:25:39,085 --> 00:25:43,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and you will realize that question cannot be answered.

255
00:25:47,471 --> 00:25:59,576
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is based on the wrong premise, because you can't watch your experience from the outside and see the one hindrance coming in, and then going away, and then... No.

256
00:25:59,656 --> 00:26:10,920
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you find yourself, because what you call you, your perspective, your discernment, your recognition, is based on the thing already being there.

257
00:26:12,157 --> 00:26:13,198
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the other way around.

258
00:26:13,838 --> 00:26:19,343
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like the you observer that's here, watching it, and then it goes away.

259
00:26:19,923 --> 00:26:30,833
[SPEAKER_00]: The observer is there because of the presence of the mental states, which means, if that mental state goes away, that observer... like, it just... it doesn't apply anymore.

260
00:26:30,973 --> 00:26:31,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

